OCR-A Axis
==========

This repository contains two kinds of material, under different terms:

  Part 1. The fonts and the font data (Richard B. Wales's terms)
  Part 2. The build tools written for this project (MIT License)

The MIT License in Part 2 does NOT apply to the fonts. Fonts produced with
these tools are derived from the Wales definition and fall under Part 1.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Part 1. The fonts and the font data
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Files:

  the font files           (OCRAAxis-VF.ttf and static/*.ttf, distributed on
                           GitHub Releases and built into dist/ by build.sh)
  skeleton-round.txt       (centerlines extracted from msk-ocra.mp)
  ocra.fea                 (OpenType features, from the OCR package)
  source/ocr-0.3.1/        (MetaPost sources, copied unmodified from the OCR package)
  ref/OCRA.ttf             (the original OCR A font from the OCR package)
  preview.png, social.png  (specimen images of the fonts)

These fonts are derived from the OCR A font in the Tsukurimashou Project's
OCR package, version 0.3.1 (https://tsukurimashou.org/ocr.php.ja), which is
Matthew Skala's MetaType1 translation of Richard B. Wales's METAFONT
definition. The OCR A outlines in this project were rebuilt from the
centerlines of that definition, with variable stroke width, corner
roundness and slant.

Matthew Skala's statement in msk-ocra.mp:

    I, Matthew Skala, release my work on this to the public domain.  However,
    the copyright notice below, from the METAFONT code I used as a source,
    may be applicable.

fntbase.mp is released to the public domain by Matthew Skala, as stated
at the start of that file.

The modifications made in this project (the stroke-based reconstruction,
the weight, roundness and slant variations, and the static instances) are
distributed under the same terms as the Wales definition, reproduced below.

The original copyright notice and credit information, reproduced intact
from msk-ocra.mp:

    METAFONT-84 Definition for OCR-A Optical Character Recognition Font
    (C) Copyright 1988, 1989 Richard B. Wales.  All Rights Reserved.

    Coded by:

        Richard B. Wales
        Computer Science Department
        University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA)
        Los Angeles, California 90024-1596 (USA)
        +1 (213) 825-5683
        wales@CS.UCLA.EDU

    Source of information:

        ANSI Standard X3.17-1977 (approved January 20, 1977, by the
        American National Standards Institute, Inc.)

    Based on an earlier version by:

        Tor Lillqvist
        Computing Services
        Technical Research Centre of Finland
        TML@FINGATE.BITNET; mcvax!santra!tml

    Terms of copyright:

        You may do anything you wish with this font definition, as
        long as you retain the original copyright notice and credit
        information intact in the source, and do not charge any fee
        for distribution of either the source or any fonts derived
        from it beyond a reasonable copying or communications charge.

    Disclaimer:

        While I have made every reasonable effort to make this font
        definition correspond to the specifications in ANSI Standard
        X3.17-1977, I do not guarantee that it conforms fully to all
        official requirements for the OCR-A font, nor that the char-
        acters produced from this definition will be usable by any
        particular OCR hardware and/or software.  It is the respon-
        sibility of the user to determine the usability of this font
        definition for his application.  Use of this font definition
        is totally at the user's own risk and without any warranty
        whatsoever, express or implied.  Neither the author nor his
        employer will be responsible for any damages, including
        secondary or consequential damages, arising out of the use of
        this font definition.

        The four ASCII characters "Tilde", "Underline", "Grave Accent",
        and "Vertical Bar" do not appear as part of ANSI X3.17-1977.
        In order to make a complete ASCII character set, I supplemented
        the standard with my own designs for these four characters.
        Since these characters are not part of any standard, they will
        presumably *NOT* be readable by any existing OCR-A equipment.

Additional disclaimer for OCR-A Axis: apart from the default instance
(wght 400, ROND 100, slnt 0), the shapes intentionally depart from
ANSI X3.17-1977 (stroke width, corners, slant, and adjustments that keep
counters open at heavy weights). They are not intended for machine
reading. The fonts are provided "as is", without warranty of any kind.


